{"timestamp": "2026-01-15 02:04:53", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f76d6fb8-a49d-43f3-98e9-027bcd8cb70e', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md",\n  "project_name": "Request Cleanup System Fix"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_014XW3PZmShwn6BLoYYyBSny', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_014XW3PZmShwn6BLoYYyBSny', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Request Cleanup System Fix - Requirements\\n\\n## Overview\\nFix the incomplete request cleanup system in ActivityTracker causing memory growth issues over long sessions.\\n\\n## Problem Statement\\nThe current ActivityTracker implementation has critical issues:\\n1. `clear_request()` method is incomplete - only has placeholder comment \\"For now, we\'ll keep the data\\"\\n2. Request-based activity filtering is not properly implemented in `get_agent_hierarchy()`\\n3. Activities accumulate indefinitely without cleanup mechanism\\n4. Memory usage grows unbounded during long sessions\\n5. No configurable retention policy for old activities\\n\\n## Objectives\\n1. Complete the `clear_request()` implementation to fully remove request data\\n2. Implement proper request-based activity filtering throughout the system\\n3. Add automatic cleanup mechanism for old activities with configurable retention\\n4. Ensure no data loss for active/recent requests\\n5. Maintain memory usage stability over long sessions\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n- Complete `clear_request()` method implementation in ActivityTracker\\n- Fix request-based filtering in `get_agent_hierarchy()` and related methods\\n- Implement automatic cleanup with configurable retention (default 24 hours for completed requests)\\n- Add cleanup mechanism for old activities (configurable, default keep 10,000 most recent)\\n- Write comprehensive tests for all cleanup functionality\\n- Ensure thread safety for cleanup operations\\n- Add logging for cleanup operations\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n- Changes to activity data structure or API\\n- UI modifications (cleanup is backend-only)\\n- Database persistence changes\\n- WebSocket notification changes\\n\\n## Requirements\\n\\n### Functional Requirements\\n\\n#### R1: Complete Request Cleanup\\n- `clear_request(request_id)` must fully remove all data associated with a request:\\n  - All activities with matching `request_id`\\n  - Agent hierarchy entries for request agents\\n  - Agent states for request agents  \\n  - Pending questions for request agents\\n  - Request entry from `requests` tracking\\n\\n#### R2: Request-Based Filtering\\n- `get_agent_hierarchy(request_id)` must properly filter by request_id when provided\\n- All methods accepting request_id must filter correctly\\n- Request_id must be properly tracked in all data structures\\n\\n#### R3: Automatic Cleanup\\n- Completed requests older than configurable retention period (default 24 hours) are auto-cleaned\\n- Failed requests older than retention period are auto-cleaned\\n- Running requests are never auto-cleaned\\n- Old activities beyond max_activities limit (default 10,000) are trimmed\\n\\n#### R4: Data Integrity\\n- No data loss for active requests\\n- No data loss for requests within retention period\\n- Cleanup operations are atomic (all-or-nothing)\\n- Parent-child agent relationships maintained during partial cleanup\\n\\n#### R5: Thread Safety\\n- Cleanup operations use proper locking mechanisms\\n- No race conditions with concurrent activity recording\\n- Safe to call cleanup from multiple threads\\n\\n#### R6: Configurability\\n- Retention period configurable (default 24 hours)\\n- Max activities limit configurable (default 10,000)\\n- Cleanup can be triggered manually or automatically\\n- Automatic cleanup interval configurable (default every hour)\\n\\n### Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n#### Performance\\n- Cleanup operations complete within 1 second for typical data volumes\\n- Memory usage remains stable over 24+ hour sessions\\n- No performance impact on activity recording during cleanup\\n\\n#### Reliability\\n- Cleanup failures are logged but don\'t crash the system\\n- Partial cleanup is handled gracefully\\n- System recovers from cleanup errors automatically\\n\\n#### Monitoring\\n- Cleanup operations are logged with details (items removed, time taken)\\n- Memory usage metrics available for monitoring\\n- Cleanup statistics available via API\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n### AC1: clear_request() Implementation\\n- [x] Method removes all activities for the request_id\\n- [x] Method removes all agent hierarchy entries for request agents\\n- [x] Method removes all agent states for request agents\\n- [x] Method removes all pending questions for request agents\\n- [x] Method removes request from requests tracking\\n- [x] Method is thread-safe with proper locking\\n- [x] Method logs cleanup details\\n\\n### AC2: Request Filtering\\n- [x] get_agent_hierarchy(request_id) returns only agents for that request\\n- [x] get_activities(request_id=X) returns only activities for request X\\n- [x] All request-based filtering works correctly\\n\\n### AC3: Automatic Cleanup\\n- [x] Old completed requests are automatically cleaned after retention period\\n- [x] Running requests are never auto-cleaned regardless of age\\n- [x] Activity list is trimmed to max_activities limit\\n- [x] Cleanup runs automatically at configured intervals\\n\\n### AC4: Data Safety\\n- [x] No active request data is lost\\n- [x] Recent request data within retention period is preserved  \\n- [x] Parent-child relationships are maintained correctly\\n- [x] Cleanup is atomic (doesn\'t leave partial state)\\n\\n### AC5: Configuration\\n- [x] Retention period is configurable\\n- [x] Max activities limit is configurable\\n- [x] Automatic cleanup can be enabled/disabled\\n- [x] Manual cleanup can be triggered\\n\\n### AC6: Memory Stability\\n- [x] Memory usage remains stable during 8+ hour test sessions\\n- [x] No memory leaks from incomplete cleanup\\n- [x] System handles cleanup of large request datasets (1000+ activities)\\n\\n## Files to Modify\\n\\n### Primary Implementation\\n- `src/runtime/agents/activity_tracker.py` - Complete cleanup implementation\\n\\n### Test Files (to be created)\\n- `tests/test_activity_tracker_cleanup.py` - Comprehensive cleanup tests\\n\\n## Test Requirements\\n\\n### Test Cases Required\\n\\n#### Unit Tests\\n1. **clear_request() functionality**\\n   - Removes all data for single request\\n   - Handles non-existent request gracefully  \\n   - Thread safety with concurrent operations\\n   - Partial cleanup scenarios\\n\\n2. **Request filtering**\\n   - get_agent_hierarchy filters correctly\\n   - get_activities filters correctly\\n   - Edge cases (empty request, no matches)\\n\\n3. **Automatic cleanup**\\n   - Old requests are cleaned\\n   - Running requests are preserved\\n   - Activity trimming works correctly\\n   - Configuration changes take effect\\n\\n#### Integration Tests\\n1. **Memory stability**\\n   - Long-running session memory usage\\n   - Large dataset cleanup performance\\n   - Concurrent request processing\\n\\n2. **Data integrity**  \\n   - No data loss during cleanup\\n   - Hierarchy relationships preserved\\n   - State consistency after cleanup\\n\\n## TDD Approach\\n1. Write failing tests first for each requirement\\n2. Implement minimum code to pass tests\\n3. Refactor while keeping tests passing\\n4. Add edge case tests and handle them\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n- All tests pass\\n- Memory usage remains stable over long sessions\\n- Cleanup operations complete quickly\\n- No data loss for active requests\\n- Existing functionality unaffected\\n- Code is well-documented and maintainable\\n\\n## Risk Mitigation\\n- Extensive testing with various data scenarios\\n- Gradual rollout with monitoring\\n- Backup/restore capability for critical data\\n- Feature flags for rollback if issues arise\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n- Current ActivityTracker API remains stable\\n- Thread safety is required for production use\\n- JSON serialization format doesn\'t change\\n- Persistence to disk continues to be supported"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_011ZVE5uczQ5REgUJoNWzPCf', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md', 'content': '# Request Cleanup System Architecture Proposal\n\n## 1. Architecture Overview\n\n### System Goal\nImplement a robust, performant, and configurable request cleanup mechanism for the ActivityTracker to manage memory usage and ensure data integrity during long-running sessions.\n\n### Architecture Pattern\n- **Layered Architecture** with clear separation of concerns\n- **Strategy Pattern** for configurable cleanup policies\n- **Thread-safe design** with concurrent access considerations\n\n## 2. Tech Stack\n\n### Language and Runtime\n- **Python 3.9+**: Leveraging modern language features\n- **Threading**: `threading` module for concurrency\n- **Logging**: `logging` module for comprehensive tracking\n\n### Core Libraries\n- `typing`: Type hinting for robust type management\n- `dataclasses`: For clean, immutable configuration objects\n- `concurrent.futures`: For potential future parallel processing\n\n## 3. System Components\n\n### Core Components\n1. **CleanupManager**\n   - Central orchestration of cleanup operations\n   - Manages retention policies\n   - Triggers automatic and manual cleanups\n   - Thread-safe implementation\n\n2. **RequestFilter**\n   - Handles request-based filtering\n   - Implements efficient filtering algorithms\n   - Supports complex multi-criteria filtering\n\n3. **CleanupStrategy**\n   - Configurable cleanup policy interface\n   - Default implementations for time-based and activity-count-based cleanup\n   - Easily extensible for future policy types\n\n### Data Structures\n- **RetentionPolicy**: Configuration for cleanup rules\n- **CleanupMetadata**: Tracks cleanup operations metadata\n- **ThreadSafeContainer**: Custom thread-safe data structure for activities\n\n## 4. Detailed Design\n\n### Cleanup Implementation Workflow\n```python\nclass CleanupManager:\n    def clear_request(self, request_id: str):\n        """\n        Atomically remove all data associated with a request\n        - Remove activities\n        - Remove agent hierarchy\n        - Remove agent states\n        - Remove pending questions\n        - Thread-safe with fine-grained locking\n        """\n        \n    def auto_cleanup(self, retention_hours: int = 24, max_activities: int = 10000):\n        """\n        Automatic cleanup mechanism\n        - Remove old completed/failed requests\n        - Trim activity list\n        - Configurable retention period\n        """\n\nclass RequestFilter:\n    def get_agent_hierarchy(self, request_id: Optional[str] = None):\n        """\n        Filter agent hierarchy by request_id\n        - Supports filtering\n        - Handles edge cases (no match, empty request)\n        """\n```\n\n## 5. Configuration Strategy\n\n### Configurability Layers\n- **Default Configuration**\n  - 24-hour retention\n  - 10,000 max activities\n- **Dynamic Configuration**\n  - Runtime configuration changes\n  - Configuration file support\n  - Environment variable overrides\n\n## 6. Thread Safety Mechanisms\n\n### Synchronization Approach\n- **Fine-grained locking**\n  - `threading.Lock()` for critical sections\n  - `RLock` for reentrant locks\n- **Atomic operations**\n- **Lock-free data structures** where possible\n\n## 7. Performance Considerations\n\n### Optimization Strategies\n- Minimize lock contention\n- Use efficient data structures\n- Lazy deletion and cleanup\n- Background thread for auto-cleanup\n\n## 8. Error Handling & Resilience\n\n### Error Management\n- Comprehensive logging\n- Graceful degradation\n- No-crash error handling\n- Detailed error reporting\n\n## 9. Monitoring & Observability\n\n### Tracking Mechanisms\n- Detailed cleanup logs\n- Performance metrics\n- Memory usage tracking\n- Cleanup operation statistics\n\n## 10. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Coverage\n- Unit tests for each component\n- Integration tests for cleanup workflows\n- Concurrency stress tests\n- Long-running session simulations\n\n## 11. Risks and Mitigations\n\n### Potential Risks\n1. **Performance Overhead**\n   - Mitigation: Lazy cleanup, background processing\n2. **Data Loss**\n   - Mitigation: Atomic operations, extensive validation\n3. **Concurrency Issues**\n   - Mitigation: Fine-grained locking, thread-safe design\n\n## 12. Future Extensibility\n\n### Potential Enhancements\n- Pluggable cleanup strategies\n- More granular filtering\n- Advanced retention policies\n- Distributed cleanup for large datasets\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis architecture provides a comprehensive, flexible solution for request cleanup, addressing all specified requirements with a focus on performance, safety, and maintainability.'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_011ZVE5uczQ5REgUJoNWzPCf', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260115_020453.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260115_020453.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 100.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.6\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The proposed architecture successfully addresses the core requirements by providing a flexible, performant solution that maintains system integrity. By focusing on lightweight implementation and clear...\n- The architecture successfully addresses the core requirements by providing a comprehensive, flexible solution for request cleanup. The design maintains system performance, ensures data integrity, and ...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current project', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create_project', 'add_task', 'update_task', 'add_note', 'get_summary', 'get_next_tasks'], 'description': 'Action to perform'}, 'project_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project ID (not needed for create_project)'}, 'project_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project name (for create_project)'}, 'description': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Description (for create_project or add_task)'}, 'task_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Task ID (for update_task)'}, 'status': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['t